Recommended stack, as adopted

This is the successor to the original stack proposal for this project. It
records what was actually built, what was rejected and why, and what is
still open. Read it alongside `_docs/01-architecture-decisions.md`, which
carries the reasoning in full.

Scope reminder: NeoCell runs on one machine. One PPE, a pool of SPEs as
child processes, no network anywhere in the job path. Everything below is
chosen with that constraint in mind.

Layer	Use	NeoCell still owns
PPE coordination	Python asyncio, subprocess	PPE scheduler, task lifecycle, worker assignment, Cell-style rules
Worker contract	Protocol Buffers over a length-prefixed pipe	The schema, its versioning, and every scheduling decision
Bulk data	Arrow IPC files, by reference above 1 MiB	Data-reference ownership, transfer decisions, workspace limits, cleanup
Scheduling concepts	Borrow ideas from Ray Core and Dask	A small single-machine scheduler, designed here
Observability	OpenTelemetry, exporting to local JSONL	Every event and measurement that gets recorded
Kernel parallelism	Not adopted; revisit only if a kernel needs it	Whether and how a worker uses internal parallelism

Use Ray and Dask as design references, not as NeoCell's runtime. Neither is
a dependency.

What was adopted, and why

Protocol Buffers, as a data format
The contract is `proto/neocell.proto` with generated Python bindings, sent
as length-prefixed messages over a child's stdin and stdout. Protobuf was
chosen for the format, not for an RPC layer: the project has no network,
so a service definition, generated stubs, and transport flow control would
all be paid for and never used. A Java worker needs a protobuf runtime, an
Arrow reader, and two file descriptors.

The schema deliberately avoids `google/protobuf/*.proto`. A bare `protoc`
install does not ship the well-known types, and depending on whatever
vendored copy sits on the machine makes builds irreproducible. Op
parameters are a typed message instead of a `Struct`, which also gives
workers compile-time checking.

Arrow IPC, with NeoCell owning lifetime
Payloads at or under 1 MiB ride inline in the message. Anything larger is
written as an Arrow IPC file and referenced by a `DataRef`, so the control
message carries a pointer rather than a payload. `BufferManager` issues the
ids, tracks size and lifetime, and deletes a file once every ref to it is
released.

The file format is chosen over the stream format specifically because it is
random-access and supports `pa.memory_map`: the reader maps the bytes
instead of copying them. Arrow supplies the representation; NeoCell owns
the ownership and scheduling semantics, which was the original intent of
this layer and is now actually implemented.

OpenTelemetry, on by default
Spans cross the process boundary using W3C `traceparent` carried in the
`exec` frame, so a task dispatched by the PPE and executed by an SPE reads
as one trace across two services. Output goes to local JSONL files and
stderr, never to a collector, because there is no network.

Telemetry is enabled by default even though it costs roughly 0.2 ms per
task. While the system is still moving, the value of having a record of
what happened outweighs the cost, and a diagnostic you must remember to
enable is one you will not have when you need it.

What was rejected

Remote RPC
Rejected outright. The project is single-machine. Cross-machine calls,
service discovery, streaming, and transport-level flow control are not
requirements, and adopting them would add a network stack, a service
definition, and a code-generation step to a path that is two pipes.

Arrow for everything
Adopted only for payloads over the inline limit. Arrow IPC for small
payloads would cost a file open and a read for data that fits in a message,
which is the opposite of an optimisation.

A shared-memory fast path
Still open, not rejected. It would remove a copy for large payloads, but it
reintroduces the coherency questions that a copied buffer avoids, and it
weakens the story for a second-language worker. Worth revisiting only with
measurements showing transfer volume actually dominating.

A kernel runtime inside an SPE
Not adopted. The first SPEs are processes, not accelerator kernels. Adding
a compiled-kernel runtime before there is a measured need would complicate
the prototype for no present benefit.

Still open

The items below were identified in the review and are queued in
`_docs/05-roadmap.md`:

- Locality-aware dispatch. The scheduler weighs whether a worker is free
  but not the cost of moving a task's input to it. This is the remaining
  gap in the Cell idea and the highest-value next step.
- Explicit task states, as a state machine with legal transitions, rather
  than a status string.
- Resource requests per task.
- Explicit backpressure, bounding the ready queue rather than relying on
  worker slots.
- Future-like result references, instead of awaiting results inline.
- Streaming a large `DataRef` through the local store in blocks, so a
  chunk need not fit the store in its entirety.

Deliberately out of scope

Named so they read as decisions rather than oversights: unified main
memory, cache coherency, a real DMA engine, MFC channel ordering,
instruction-level timing, and any claim about PS3 or Cell performance.
`neocell` demonstrates an architecture; it does not model hardware.